      "path": "cordis.patch.yml",
      "content": "# dsh-windows-mcp — bundle patch layer\n#\n# This package is a CARRIER bundle: it ships no service of its own, it only\n# mounts two loader rows in the DSH profile.\n#\n#   1. `windows-mcp-plugin`  → this package's host half (lib/index.js). It adds\n#      one diagnostic tool (`windows_mcp_doctor`) and nothing else.\n#   2. `windows-mcp-bridge`  → the OFFICIAL in-box MCP client bridge shipped by\n#      DeepSeek Harness itself (`@deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-client`). It lives inside\n#      app.asar and is resolved by the loader from the Desktop install anchor;\n#      a third-party plugin must NOT try to import it.\n#\n# Every tool the bridge registers shows up as `mcp__windows__<ToolName>`.\n#\n# BOOT SAFETY — the bridge row ships `disabled: true` on purpose.\n#\n# The fallback command below is `uvx windows-mcp@0.8.8`, which resolves and\n# downloads Windows-MCP's Python dependencies from the package index. On a slow\n# or blocked network that resolve can sit inside DSH's startup for minutes and\n# the window stays on \"Loading plugins…\". A plugin must never be able to do that\n# to the host, so the row ships inert: DSH boots, the Plugins page says the\n# dependency is missing, and the user starts the install from there (it probes\n# mirrors and installs into the plugin's own toolchain directory).\n#\n# The installer then writes the row again into the profile's own patch layer\n# ($DSH_HOME/profiles/<profile>/cordis.patch.yml) between the\n# `# >>> dsh-windows-mcp …` markers, with `disabled: false` and `command:`\n# pointing at the installed executable — so later boots spawn the exe directly\n# and never touch the network or `uvx`.\n#\n# Tuning: change `args` / `env` below, or override this row from your profile's\n# own patch layer:\n#\n#   - id: windows-mcp-bridge\n#     disabled: false\n#     config:\n#       command: 'C:\\path\\to\\windows-mcp.exe'\n#       args:\n#         - serve\n#         - --exclude-tools\n#         - App\n#\n- insert:\n    - id: windows-mcp-plugin\n      name: dsh-windows-mcp\n\n    - id: windows-mcp-bridge\n      name: \"@deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-client\"\n      # Inert until the installer points it at a real executable (see above):\n      # a cold `uvx` resolve inside DSH's startup can hang the whole window.\n      disabled: true\n      config:\n        # Namespace for every tool name: mcp__windows__Screenshot, ...\n        serverName: windows\n        transport: stdio\n        # Only reached if someone re-enables this row without rewriting\n        # `command`; harmless, because by then the toolchain is installed.\n        command: uvx\n        args:\n          # Pinned for reproducibility. Replace with `windows-mcp` to float, or\n          # with an absolute path to a fixed interpreter (`python -m windows_mcp`).\n          - windows-mcp@0.8.8\n          - serve\n          # Curation: drop the tools DSH already covers (its own shell and file\n          # tools), plus `App`, which upstream only supports reliably on an\n          # English Windows install. Never list `ControlStatus` here — upstream\n          # keeps it unconditionally.\n          - --exclude-tools\n          - App,PowerShell,FileSystem\n        # Screenshots, UIA tree walks and PowerShell-style waits are slow.\n        # Keep the pinned version in `args` below: the settings page and the\n        # installer read it, and the profile block the installer writes restates\n        # this row at that version.\n        toolCallTimeoutMs: 120000\n        # Belt and braces: a missing Windows-MCP must never fail the profile.\n        failOnStartupError: false\n        reconnect:\n          enabled: true\n          initialDelayMs: 500\n          maxDelayMs: 30000\n          maxAttempts: 10\n        env:\n          # Upstream defaults to PostHog telemetry ON; a desktop-automation\n          # plugin should not phone home.\n          ANONYMIZED_TELEMETRY: \"false\"\n          # Suppress the orange \"control taken over\" flash on every screenshot.\n          WINDOWS_MCP_DISABLE_FLASH: \"1\"\n          # Downscale screenshots (0.1–1.0) to cut image tokens. Lower it to\n          # 0.5 if your budget is tight; raise it if small UI text is unreadable.\n          WINDOWS_MCP_SCREENSHOT_SCALE: \"0.75\"\n"
